==English==
{{was wotd|2026|June|26}}
[[File:Feuer entzünden 2011-by-RaBoe-18.jpg|thumb|[[wood#Noun|Wood]] being inflamed ''({{senseno|en|to burn}})'' in a [[fireplace]].]]

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰel-|id=shiny}}
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:enflaumen|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|inflammen}}, {{m|enm|enflamen}}, {{m|enm|enflaumen|t=to set (something) on fire; to be on fire; (''figurative'') to enrage (someone); to become enraged; to incite (someone) to do evil; to inspire (someone); (''medicine'') to cause inflammation of (a body part)}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=enflaumen|pos=v|id=MED13775}}</ref> from {{der|en|fro|enflamer}}, {{m|fro|enflammer|t=to set (something) on fire, inflame; to be on fire; (''figurative'') to fuel (a debate, hatred, etc.)}} (modern {{cog|fr|enflammer}}), from {{der|en|la|inflammāre}}, the {{lg|present}} {{lg|active}} {{lg|infinitive}} of {{m|la|inflammō|t=to set (something) on fire, inflame; (''figurative'') to excite (someone)}}, from {{m|la|in-|pos={{lg|prefix}} meaning ‘into; to, towards’}} + {{m|la|flammō|t=to set (something) on fire; to be on fire; (''figurative'') to incite (someone)}}<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=1797530247|date=March 2026|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> (from {{m|la|flamma|t=flame, fire; (''figurative'') passion}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰel-|*bʰel-, *bʰl̥g-|to blaze, burn; to glow, shine; bright flame, fire}}) + {{m|la|-ō|pos=suffix forming regular first-{{lg|conjugation}} {{lg|verb}}s}}). {{surf|en|in-|pos1=prefix meaning ‘into; to, towards’|flame}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈfleɪm/|[ɪɱ-]|[-ˈfleɪ̯m]|a=RP,GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-inflame.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪm|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|in|flame}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## {{senseid|en|to burn}} To [[set#Verb|set]] (someone or something) on [[fire#Noun|fire]]; to [[cause#Verb|cause]] (someone or something) to [[burn#Verb|burn]], [[flame#Verb|flame]], or [[glow#Verb|glow]]; to [[kindle#Verb|kindle]].
##: {{syn|en|aflame#Verb|ignite|Thesaurus:kindle}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1579|author=Iohn Auenar [''i.e.'', {{w|Johann Habermann}}]|chapter=A Praier against Desperation|translator=[[w:Thomas Rogers (priest)|Thomas Rogers]]|title=The Enimie of Securitie or A Dailie Exercise of Godly Meditations,{{nb...|Drawne out of the Pure Fountaines of the Holie Scriptures, and Published for the Profite of Al Persons of Any State or Calling, in the German and Latine Tonges, by the Right Reuerende Maister Iohn Auenar, Publike Professor of the Hebrue Tonge, in the Famour Vniuersitie of Witeberge; in Englishe by Thomas Rogers Maister of Artes and Student in Diuinitie. Newlie Corrected and Augmented.}}|location=[London]|page=281|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_the-enimie-of-securitie-_habermann-j_1579/page/281/mode/1up|oclc=1261648034|passage={{quote-gloss|{{w|Jesus}}, you}} art the eternal Orator, vvhich both outvvardlie talkeſt to mankinde by thy Goſpel, and invvardlie in our harts '''inflameſt''' thy light vvhich is true confeſſion, faith, and comfort in thee: {{...}}|footer={{small|A figurative use.}}}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare King John|act=V|scene=i|page=18|column=1|passage=Novv keep your holy vvord, go meet the ''French'', / And from his holineſſe vſe all your povver / To ſtop their marches 'fore vve are '''enflam'd''': / Our diſcontented Counties doe reuolt: / Our people quarrell vvith obedience, / Svvearing allegiance, and the loue of ſoule / To ſtranger-bloud, to forren Royalty; {{...}}|footer=A figurative use.}}
##* {{RQ:Homer Chapman Iliads|book=VIII|page=114|passage=[W]e ſhould haue made retreate, / By light of the '''inflamed''' fleet, vvith all the Greeks eſcheate; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|chapter=Experiment Solitary, Touching Subterrany Fires|para=361|page=97|passage=It is reported by one of the ''Ancients'', that in Part of ''Media'', there are ''Eruptions'' of ''Flames'' out of ''Plaines''; {{...}} There be alſo ſome ''Blinde Fires'', vnder ''Stone'', vvhich flame not out, but ''Oile'' being povvred vpon them, they flame out. The Cauſe vvhereof is, for that it ſeemeth, the ''Fire'' is ſo choaked, as not able to remoue the Stone, it is ''Heat'', rather than ''Flame''; VVhich neuertheleſſe is ſufficient to '''Enflame''' the ''Oyle''.}}
##* {{RQ:Heywood Elizabeth|chapter=La{{quote-gloss|dy}} Elizabeth almost Burned in Her Bed|page=167|passage=[T]he vvhole Kingdome vvas then '''enflamed''' vvith ''Bonefires'' of [[w:God in Christianity|Gods]] Saints, there vvas Fire in the Center, Fire all about the Circũference {{quote-gloss|circumference}}, Fire at home, Fire abroad; Fire in her priuate Chamber, Fire all ouer the vvhole Kingdome; {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1664|author=Thomas of Kempis [''i.e.'', {{w|Thomas à Kempis}}]|chapter=Of the Apostles Preaching, and Publishing the Most Holy Name of Our Sauiour Iesus Christ for Mans Saluation All the World ouer|translator=Thomas Carre [pseudonym; {{w|Miles Pinkney}}]|title=Meditations and Prayers of the Life, Passion, Resvrrection and Ascension of Ovr Saviovr Iesvs Christ.{{nb...|Written in Latine by Thomas of Kempis Canon Regular of the Order of S[aint] Augustine, True Authour of The Following of Christ. And Englished by Thomas Carre Confessour to the Canonesses Regular of S. Augustins Order of N[otre] Dame de Sion.}}|location=Paris|publisher={{...|Printed by}} V[erlet] dv Movtier|page=603|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_meditations-and-prayers_thomas-kempis_1664/page/603/mode/1up|oclc=913036192|passage=[T]hou {{quote-gloss|{{w|Jesus}}}} on a ſoudaine '''inflamedſt''' the rude mynds of the Apoſtles vvith the fire of the [[w:Holy Spirit in Christianity|holy Ghoſt]]; {{...}}|footer={{small|A figurative use.}}}}
##* {{RQ:T. Herbert Travaile|edition=4th|chapter=[Law Rigidly Executed]|page=211|passage=[F]orty Camels entring loaden vvith Tobacco out of ''India'', (the drivers being ignorant of a late prohibition, the King ſometimes commanding and reſtraining as reaſon of State invited,) ''Mamet Ally-beg'' {{...}} offering vvithal to his angry Juſtice a diſmal ſacrifice of forty load of Tobacco vvhich vvas put into a deep hole that ſerved as a pipe, and being '''inflamed''', in a black vapour gave the Citizens ''gratis'' for tvvo vvhole days and nights an unpleaing incenſe.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1700|author=&#91;[[w:John Austin (1613–1669)|John Austin]]&#93;|chapter=w:Psalm 46|editor=[[w:George Hickes (divine)|George Hickes]]|title=Devotions in the Ancient Way of Offices. With Psalms, Hymns, and Prayers for Every Day of the Week, and Every Holiday in the Year.{{nb...|Reformed by a Person of Quality, and Published by George Hickes, D.D.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} J. James{{nb...|at the Bell, in St. Paul’s Church-Yard.}}|page=168|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_devotions-in-the-ancient_1700/page/168/mode/1up|oclc=1597688138|passage=Thou {{quote-gloss|[[w:God in Christianity|God]]}} didſt verifie their {{quote-gloss|the Apostles'}} Miſſion vvith the povver of Miracles; and '''enflamedſt''' their Hearts vvith the Fire of thy [[w:Holy Spirit in Christianity|Spirit]].|footer={{small|A figurative use.}}}}
## {{lb|en|by extension}}
##: {{syn|en|irritate}}
### To [[cause#Verb|cause]] (something) to appear [[bright#Adjective|bright]] or [[red#Adjective|red]], as if on fire; to [[brighten]], to [[redden]].
###* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|book=4|lines=576–579|page=138|passage=I vvill my ſelf conduct thee on thy VVay, / VVhen next the Southing Sun '''inflames''' the Day; / VVhen the dry Herbage thirſts for Devvs in vain, / And Sheep, in Shades, avoid the parching Plain.}}
###* {{RQ:Shelley Poetical Works|edition=revised|volume=II|poem=Charles I|scene=i|page=376|passage=Even now we see the redness of the torches / '''Inflame''' the night to the eastward, and the clarions / Gasp (?) to us on the wind's wave.}}
###* {{RQ:Ballard Dream Company|edition=US|chapter=Stark's Zoo|page=40|passage=Along the perimeter road the police car approached, headlamps  '''inflaming''' the afternoon sunlight.}}
### To cause (the [[body#Noun|body]] or a [[body part]]) to [[feel#Verb|feel]] [[abnormally]] or [[unusually]] [[hot#Adjective|hot]]; specifically {{lb|en|medicine}}, to cause [[inflammation]], including [[abnormal#Adjective|abnormal]] [[heat#Noun|heat]], [[pain#Noun|pain]], [[redness]], [[swelling#Noun|swelling]], etc., in (the body or a body part).
###: {{ux|en|to '''inflame''' the eyes by overwork}}
###* {{RQ:Palsgrave Lesclarcissement|book=3rd|chapter=The Table of Verbes|folio=ccxxv|column=1|passage=His lyver is al '''enflamed''' with drinkyng of hote wynes: {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Geneva Bible|book=Isaiah|chapter=V|verse=11|folio=284|verso=1|column=1|passage=VVo vnto them, that riſe vp early to follovve drunkennes, and to them that cõtinue {{quote-gloss|continue}} vntil night, ''til'' the vvine do '''inflame''' them.}}
###* {{RQ:Topsell Foure-footed Beastes|chapter=Of the Oxe and Cow|page=77|passage=There be alſo hearbes vvhich increaſe in cattell diuers diſeaſes, as {{...}} VVolfe-bane, vvhich is that graſſe in ''cilicia'', vvhich '''inflameth''' oxen, {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:T. Herbert Travaile|edition=4th|chapter=[Trees. Dodo's.]|page=382|passage=[I]n curioſity I put ſome of the vvood in my mouth and chevved it; vvhat the vertue may be I knovv not; but for half an hour my mouth vvas '''inflamed''' as if I had taken ſo much Vitriol.}}
###* {{RQ:Swift Journal to Stella|letter=XV|date=24 April 1712|page=299|passage='Tis this day just a month since I felt the pain on the tip of my left shoulder which grew worse, and spread for six days; then broke all out by my collar, and left side of my neck in monstrous red spots '''inflamed''', and these grew to small pimples.}}
###* {{RQ:Pope Rape of the Lock|year=1717|canto=IV|page=152|passage=But oh! if e'er thy ''Gnome'' could ſpoil a grace, / Or raiſe a pimple on a beauteous face; / Like Citron-vvaters matrons cheeks '''inflame''', / Or change complexions at a loſing game; {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Tennyson Princess|page=15|passage={{...}} I saw my father's face / Grow long and troubled like a rising moon, / '''Inflamed''' with wrath: {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:F. Marryat Vampire|edition=copyright|chapter=XV|page=264|passage=Her eyes were '''inflamed''' with crying, and she shook like an aspen leaf.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}}
### To cause (someone, or their [[heart#Noun|heart]], [[mind#Noun|mind]], etc.) to feel [[strong#Adjective|strong]] [[desire#Noun|desire]] or other [[feeling#Noun|feelings]]; to [[excite]] (someone) to [[great#Adjective|great]] [[passion#Noun|passion]]; specifically, to cause (someone) to feel [[anger#Noun|anger]] or [[rage#Noun|rage]]; to [[anger#Verb|anger]], to [[enrage]], to [[incense#Verb|incense]].
###: {{sense|to cause anger}} {{syn|en|exasperate|irritate|Thesaurus:enrage}}
###: {{ant|en|disinflame<q:rare>}}
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|act=III|scene=ii|page=122|column=1|passage=You are not VVood, you are not Stones, but men: / And being men, hearing the VVill of ''Cæſar'', / It vvill '''inflame''' you, it vvill make you mad; {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Butler Hudibras|part=1|page=87|passage=Honour, Revenge, Contempt and Shame, / Did equally their breaſts '''enflame'''.}}
###* {{RQ:Mather Invisible World|chapter=Enchantments Encountred|page=29|passage=To ''VVrangle the Devil'', out of the Country, vvill be truly a Nevv Experiment! Alas, vve are not Avvare of the ''Devil'', if vve do not think, that he aims at '''Enflaming''' us one againſt another; & ſhall vve ſuffer our ſelves to be ''Devil-Ridden''? or, by any ''Vnadviſableneſs'', contribute unto the VVidening of our Breaches?}}
###* {{RQ:Dryden et al Examen Poeticum|title=Veni Creator Spiritus|stanza=4|page=308|passage=Refine and purge our Earthly Parts; / But, oh, '''inflame''' and fire our Hearts!}}
###* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|book=12|lines=472–474|page=592|passage=VVhat ſudden Rage, vvhat nevv Deſire of Blood / '''Inflames''' your alter'd Minds? O ''Trojans'' ceaſe / From impious Arms, nor violate the Peace.}}
###* {{RQ:Hume Moral and Political|volume=I|page=126|passage=[T]he former Party inclin'd moſt to the Republican Part of our Government, and the latter to the Monarchial. In vvhich reſpect they may be conſider'd as Court and Country-Party '''enflam'd''' into a Civil VVar, by an unhappy Concurrence of Circumſtances, and by the turbulent Spirit of the Age.}}
###* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=XXIII|page=181|passage=To Edward {{...}} he was terrible, nerve-'''inflaming''', poisonously asphyxiating. He sat rocking himself in the late Mr. Churchill's swing chair, smoking and twaddling.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|year=2022|author=w:Tina Brown|chapter=The Lost Boys: How the Princes Survived Their Childhood|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=F6BEEAAAQBAJ&pg=PT156|title=The Palace Papers: Inside the House of Windsor—the Truth and the Turmoil|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Crown Publishing Group|Crown]]|section=part 2|isbn=978-0-593-13809-0|passage=Even though no one had known about the plan in advance, the paparazzi were waiting at the door as they left the nightclub. [[w:Gulu Lalvani|{{quote-gloss|Gulu}} Lalvani]] told me, "Whether Harry's Bar called them or she tipped them off, I don't know.” (I think we do&nbsp;…) He realizes now that she was using him to '''inflame''' the true object of her affections, {{w|Hasnat Khan}}. The pictures of Lalvani and Diana that appeared the next day were the whole point.}}
### To excite or [[intensify]] (a feeling, such as an [[appetite]] or passion), especially to an [[excessive]] or [[unnatural#Adjective|unnatural]] [[level#Noun|level]].
###: {{syn|en|aflame#Verb|arouse|kindle|provoke|Thesaurus:incite|Thesaurus:thrill}}
###: {{ux|en|to '''inflame''' desire}}
###* {{RQ:T. Herbert Travaile|chapter=A Later Tragicall History of the Georgians, Christians|pages=72–73|pageref=72|passage=Yet ſome report, by caſualtie of riding, or like accident, his bodie in ſome ſort became imperfect. VVhich notvvithſtanding quenched not, but rather '''inflamed''' his daring courage, maliciously looking vpon the vertue and perfections of other mens minds and bodies.}}
###* {{RQ:Prescott Ferdinand and Isabella|volume=II|chapter=Attempted Assassination of Ferdinand.—Return and Second Voyage of [[w:Christopher Columbus|Columbus]]. 1492–1493.|page=178|passage=The reports and sanguine speculations of the first adventures had '''inflamed''' the cupidity of many, {{...}}}}
### To cause (something [[intangible#Adjective|intangible]], such as a [[dispute#Noun|dispute]] or [[violence#Noun|violence]], or [[hunger#Noun|hunger]] or [[thirst#Noun|thirst]]) to [[become]] more [[serious#Adjective|serious]] or [[bad#Adjective|worse]]; to [[aggravate#Verb|aggravate]], to [[exacerbate]].
###* {{RQ:Tatler|date=21 January 1709|author=Addison|issue=123|page=251|passage=They often drank of this stream, which had such a particular quality in it, that though it refreshed them for a time, it rather '''inflamed''' than quenched their thirst.}}
###* {{RQ:Spectator|date=7 June 1712|author=Addison|issue=399|page=511|passage=A friend exaggerates a man's virtues, an enemy '''inflames''' his crimes.}}
###* {{quote-web|en|date=25 August 2017|title=Arrest Threat as {{w|Yingluck Shinawatra}} Misses Verdict|work=[[w:Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20211027031645/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2017/8/25/arrest-threat-as-yingluck-shinawatra-misses-verdict|passage=The long-awaited verdict could '''inflame''' tension in the Southeast Asian country and have far-reaching implications in the politically divided kingdom.}}
### {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[increase#Verb|increase]] (the [[price#Noun|price]] or [[quantity]] of something).
###* {{RQ:Tatler|date=9 February 1710|issue=131|page=283|passage=[T]his new corporation of druggists had '''inflamed''' the bills of mortality, and puzzled the college of physicians with diseases, for which they neither knew a name or cure.}}
###* {{RQ:Goldsmith She Stoops to Conquer|page=20|passage=As you ſay, vve paſſengers are to be taxed to pay all theſe fineries. I have often ſeen a good ſideboard, or a marble chimney-piece, tho' not actually put in the bill, '''enflame''' a reckoning confoundedly.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## To [[burst#Verb|burst]] into [[flame#Noun|flames]], to [[catch#Verb|catch]] fire.
##* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|chapter=Experiment in Consort, Touching the Continuance of Flame|para=373|page=100|passage=[A]s the ''Oile'' vvaſteth, and deſcendeth, ſo the Top of the ''Turret'', by little and little, filleth vvith Aire; vvhich is cauſed by the Rarefaction of the Oile by the Heat. It vvere vvorthy the Obſeruation, to make a Hole, in the Top of the ''Turret'', and to trie, vvhen the ''Oile'' is almoſt conſumed, vvhether the ''Aire'' made of the ''Oile'', if you put to it a ''Flame'' of a ''Candle'', in the letting of it forth, vvill '''Enflame'''.}}
## {{lb|en|by extension}}
### Of a [[person#Noun|person]]'s body or body part: to feel abnormally or unusually hot; specifically {{lb|en|medicine}}, to be [[affect#Verb|affected]] by inflammation.
###* {{RQ:Topsell Foure-footed Beastes|chapter=Of the Fox [The Crvcigeran Foxe]|page=224|passage=[S]ometime the liuer of the Fox '''inflameth''', and then it is not cured but by the vlcerous blood flovving to the skin, and that euill blood cauſeth the ''Alopecia'', or falling avvay of the haire, for vvhich cauſe (as is already ſaid) a Foxes skin is little vvorth that is taken in the ſummer time.}}
### {{lb|en|obsolete}} To become very hot.
###* {{RQ:T. Herbert Travaile|chapter=[Choul. Mangalor.]|page=296|passage=The vvind vvas favourable a vvhile; but ere long becalmed, vvhereby the ayre '''inflam'd''', and Sea gave a fierie reflection; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} To feel strong desire or other feelings; to be [[passionate#Adjective|passionate]]; specifically, to feel anger or rage.
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|lines=790–791|sig=G2|verso=1|page=53|passage=I fled, but he {{quote-gloss|Death}} purſu'd (though more, it ſeems, / '''Inflam'd''' vvith luſt then rage) and ſvvifter far, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Quarles Hadassa|section=7|subsection=Meditatio sexta|sig=F3|verso=1|page=50|passage=[W]hen the ſeruants of the King had ſeene, / Their fell diſdaine, mixt vvith an enuious ſpleene, / '''Inflam'd'''; They queſtion'd hovv he durſt vvithſtand / The iuſt performance of the Kings Command: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Carlyle Schiller|chapter=Note B. Page 42. Letters of Schiller|page=337|passage=I know how soon your noble heart '''inflames''' when sympathy and humanity appeal to it; I know how strong your courage is to undertake a noble action, and how warm your zeal to finish it.}}
##* {{RQ:Roethke Open House|poem=Feud|page=5|passage=You meditate upon the nerves, / '''Inflame''' with hate. This ancient feud / Is seldom won. {{...}}}}

